; Arithmetic between a vector/tensor and a SCALAR fails the same way in both
; operand orders, on every substrate.
;
; Element-wise arithmetic is a binary contract: two operands of matching shape
; (docs/reference/tensors/operations.md), with scalar broadcast spelled as its
; own operator (`tensor-scale`). A vector against a scalar is therefore a type
; error — and the point of this entry is that it is the SAME type error whichever
; side the vector is on, and that it is CATCHABLE rather than fatal.
;
; It used not to be. Native codegen chose its element-wise kernel from the LEFT
; operand alone, so a vector on the left was handed to the Scheme-vector kernel,
; which read the scalar as `[len:i64][tagged elems...]`:
;
;   (* (vector 1 2) 2)   native  SIGSEGV at 0xa, exit 139, no diagnostic
;                        vm      error: *: expected numeric operands (catchable)
;   (+ (vector 1 2) 1)   native  SIGSEGV at 0x9, exit 139, no diagnostic
;   (* 2 (vector 1 2))   native  Type error in tensor-mul: expected tensor, …
;                        vm      error: *: expected numeric operands
;
; One operator, two answers, decided by which operand happened to be written
; first — and the wrong one was a fatal signal, which no `guard` can intercept
; and no consumer can distinguish from a compiler bug. Native now classifies
; BOTH operand positions before dereferencing either, so the two orders raise
; the same catchable error and the VM's long-standing behaviour is matched
; rather than diverged from.
;
; Only the rows both engines agree on live here. The VM has no vector/tensor
; arithmetic under `+ - * /` at all (it raises for `(* (vector 1 2) (vector 3 4))`
; too, which native answers element-wise), so the working element-wise results
; and the mixed vector/tensor product are gated natively in
; tests/features/vector_scalar_arithmetic_dispatch_test.esk instead. The
; unguarded form's fail-closed exit status is gated in
; tests/vm_parity/fatal/vector_scalar_arith.esk.

; ---- a vector on the LEFT, a scalar on the right: catchable, not fatal ----
(display (guard (e (#t "caught")) (* (vector 1 2) 2))) (newline)
(display (guard (e (#t "caught")) (+ (vector 1 2) 1))) (newline)
(display (guard (e (#t "caught")) (- (vector 1 2) 1))) (newline)
(display (guard (e (#t "caught")) (/ (vector 2 4) 2))) (newline)

; ---- inexact elements take the same route ----
(display (guard (e (#t "caught")) (* (vector 1.0 2.0) 2))) (newline)
(display (guard (e (#t "caught")) (* (vector 1.0 2.0) 2.0))) (newline)

; ---- the other operand order agrees ----
(display (guard (e (#t "caught")) (* 2 (vector 1 2)))) (newline)
(display (guard (e (#t "caught")) (+ 1 (vector 1 2)))) (newline)
(display (guard (e (#t "caught")) (- 1 (vector 1 2)))) (newline)
(display (guard (e (#t "caught")) (/ 2 (vector 2 4)))) (newline)

; ---- through a function: the numeric path first, then the vector call site ----
(define (scale x m) (* x m))
(display (scale 3 4)) (newline)
(display (guard (e (#t "caught")) (scale (vector 1 2) 2))) (newline)
(display (scale 5 6)) (newline)

; ---- a list is not a vector and raises as it always did ----
(display (guard (e (#t "caught")) (* (list 1 2) 2))) (newline)

; ---- scalar arithmetic is untouched ----
(display (* 6 7)) (newline)
(display (+ 6 7)) (newline)
(display (* 2 3.5)) (newline)
